---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/names.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const p = data.people[0];
const groups = [
  ['forename', 'Her forename'],
  ['maiden', 'Her maiden surname'],
  ['married', 'Her married surnames'],
];
---
<Base title={data.title} subtitle={data.dek}
  lede="An index cannot be searched for a person. It can only be searched for a spelling. This page exists because this archive learned that the expensive way: a file in which Ivone is the plaintiff sat unfound for weeks behind two letters.">

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Why this page exists</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Ivone is recorded under six spellings of her maiden surname across five documents</strong>,
      three forenames and four married surnames. That is not unusual for a Portuguese woman in an
      English-and-Afrikaans record system. What is unusual is how much has turned on it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>TAB WLD 2545/1950 is the only file in which she is the plaintiff</strong> &mdash; the
      one in which she sued Kurt rather than being sued. It went unfound through months of searching
      because the index carries her as <strong>IVONE LUWENSKI, born FIGARETTO</strong>. Neither word
      is a spelling a person would choose. A search on the correct name could never have found it, and
      did not.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      Every form below is given with the record it comes from. Where a spelling appears only in an
      index and in no document, that is said plainly &mdash; the difference matters, because an index
      corruption tells you about the cataloguer and a document variant tells you about the family.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>{p.canonical} <span class="tag doc">b. {p.born}</span></h3>
    <p class="small">{p.why}</p>
    {groups.map(([kind, label]) => (
      <div>
        <h4>{label}</h4>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>as written</th><th>where it appears</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {p.forms.filter((f) => f.kind === kind).map((f) => (
              <tr>
                <td><strong>{f.form}</strong></td>
                <td class="small">{f.where}</td>
                <td class="small muted">{f.note}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The rule this archive now works to</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>No surname search counts as done until every recorded spelling has been tried.</strong>
      Applied to the Union Gazette on 16 September 2026, that rule turned a three-spelling sweep into
      a six-spelling one and made the resulting negative worth stating. Applied to NAAIRS, it shows
      that <strong>FIGARETTO, LUWENSKI and LUWINSKY each return exactly one record &mdash; and each of
      those records is invisible to the correct spelling.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      It also cuts the other way, which is the part worth remembering.
      <strong>FIGARETTO and LUWENSKI appear nowhere in any African gazette at all.</strong> They exist
      only inside the archival index. So a spelling that finds a file can still be a spelling nobody
      ever wrote &mdash; useful for searching, useless as evidence about the family.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>{data.tested_title} <span class="tag doc">16 Sep 2026</span></h3>
    <p class="small">{data.tested_dek}</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Forty-one spellings were put to the indexes</strong> &mdash; twenty-one ways of writing
      Figueiredo and twenty of writing Luwinski, including many that appear in no record and were
      invented on purpose to see whether a clerk had ever made the same slip.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>spelling</th><th>NAAIRS</th><th>gendatabase</th><th>what it uniquely surfaced</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.tested.map((t) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{t.form}</strong></td>
            <td class="small">{t.naairs}</td>
            <td class="small">{t.gendb}</td>
            <td class="small muted">{t.unique}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="small muted">
      A dash means the spelling was not put to that index rather than that it returned nothing.
      <strong>The distinction is the whole point of this archive&rsquo;s negatives</strong> and it is
      not going to be blurred here.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What the exercise was actually worth</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>One genuinely new record, and it is a good one.</strong> Estate
      <strong>TAB MHG 7029/54</strong> belongs to a Portuguese man who died in Johannesburg in 1954.
      The Union Gazette advertises him as <strong>FIGUEIREDO, Agostinho Vicente Sebastiao</strong>.
      NAAIRS files him as <strong>FIQUEIREDO</strong>. Two indexes, two spellings, one man &mdash; and
      until 16 September 2026 nobody had ever typed the one with a Q.
    </p>
    <p>
      He is <em>not</em> claimed as a relative. Figueiredo is a common Portuguese surname and nothing
      connects him to Ivone but a city and a decade. <strong>What his file carries is a death notice,
      and a death notice names parents</strong> &mdash; which is the one thing this archive cannot get
      for her.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And one real exclusion.</strong> Kurt came out of East Prussia, and the slide from
      <em>Luwinski</em> to <em>Lewinski</em> is exactly the slide a clerk makes. There is a Lewinski
      and a Lewinsky family in Johannesburg in the same decades &mdash; Erwin, Bella born Berman,
      Meinhard, Ingeborg Ursula born Leske. <strong>They are not this family.</strong> That is worth
      as much as a find, and it is recorded here so that it is never re-opened as a hopeful
      possibility.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The same problem in the other lines</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>name</th><th>forms</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.others.map((o) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{o.name}</strong></td>
            <td class="small">{o.forms}</td>
            <td class="small muted">{o.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="small muted">
    See also <a href={base + '/ivone/'}>Ivone</a>,
    <a href={base + '/negatives/'}>the bounded negatives</a>, and
    <a href={base + '/sources/'}>sources</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
